= Discussion <discussion>

This chapter interprets the evaluation findings on a conceptual level, discusses their implications for practical application of stage decomposition and identifies methodological limitations.

The evaluation demonstrates that stage decomposition can substantially improve solution quality on large, complex scenarios where monolithic solving struggles. However, the approach exhibits fundamental structural limitations that stem from its two-phase design and underlying nature of decomposition.

#heading(level: 2, outlined: false)[Interpretation of Findings]

@finding-local-optimal states that decomposed runs fail to find the global optimum even when all subproblems are solved to optimality. @finding-structural-limitation identifies the root cause as suboptimal inter-subnetwork flow commitments during the bottom-up phase. Together, these findings point to a fundamental information asymmetry problem inherent in the hierarchical decomposition approach because of binding decisions by the bottom-up about material flows without visibility into upstream capacity constraints. The underlying issue is that stage decomposition breaks the global coordination that monolithic solving provides. Classical decomposition methods like Dantzig-Wolfe @dantzig1960 and Benders decomposition @benders1962 address this through iterative information exchange between master and subproblems. Lagrangian relaxation @fisher1981 uses iterative price signals to coordinate decoupled subproblems. The stage decomposition trades this iterative coordination for computational simplicity and speed, accepting that some global optimality will be lost. Two possible improvements to mitigate this limitation are discussed in @improvement-of-assymmetry.

@finding-big-scenarios states that decomposition provides good improvement on large scenarios solved far from optimality by monolithic runs. This observation aligns with the super-linear scaling behavior of mixed-integer programming solvers @wolsey2020. As problem size increases, monolithic solve time grows faster than linearly due to the combinatorial explosion in the branch-and-bound tree. Decomposition counteracts this by partitioning the problem into smaller subproblems whose combined solve times remain smaller than the monolithic solve time. The practical implication is that decomposition should primarily be applied to scenarios that are too large for monolithic solving to reach good solutions within the available time budget. For smaller scenarios that can be solved optimally by monolithic approaches, the potential optimality loss might not be justified. However, @finding-well-structured identifies an alternative justification for using the approach also on selective small scenarios.

@finding-well-structured identifies that stage-aligned supply chains can also enable solution improvements by decomposition, because it produces similar sized problems and reduces the information loss at subnetwork boundaries. The practical implication is that decomposition should be used for scenarios that exhibit this favorable topology.

@finding-mono-infeasible shows that decomposition can provide usable solutions in cases where the monolithic solver fails to find any feasible solution within the given time budget. Notably, decomposition obtains a solution that is optimal with respect to the individual decomposed subproblems, while the monolithic approach finds no solution at all. The decomposition decouples constraints that prevent the monolithic solver from making progress. This introduces a practical use case for decomposition as a fallback strategy. When monolithic solving fails to produce any result within the allocated time, enabling decomposition offers an alternative path to obtaining a solution rather than requiring an increase in the runtime budget.

@finding-production-dominance conversely shows that production-dominated scenarios perform poorly under decomposition. This is expected because decomposition benefits diminish when one subproblem dominates the overall complexity. The practical implication is that decomposition should only be applied when problem complexity is reasonably distributed across stages. Scenarios where transportation decisions are also modeled as discrete variables tend to exhibit more balanced complexity. Conversely, production scenarios where procurement is trivial and distribution is continuous should avoid this decomposition approach entirely.

@finding-topdown-bottleneck identifies that top-down runs consistently require more time than bottom-up runs despite similar complexity metrics, likely due to problem degeneracy. This finding reveals an unintended consequence of the virtual demand mechanism. During bottom-up, virtual demand variables act as slack variables that provide flexibility in satisfying link node inventory balance constraints, because these variables are not part of any other constraint and can be chosen freely. Their removal in top-down creates a more constrained system where balance must be achieved through tightly coupled production and transportation variables. The mathematical interpretation is that the feasible polyhedron becomes more degenerate when independent slack variables are replaced with dependent variables participating in multiple constraints. Degenerate linear programs are known to cause numerical difficulties for simplex-based methods and Crossover @bertsimas1997, explaining the observed solver behavior. A potential improvement using Dual Simplex warmstarting is discussed in @improvement-degeneracy.

To sum up, decomposition reaches its best achievable solution faster than monolithic solving reaches the same quality level, unless it encounters the degeneracy problems observed in several scenarios. Moreover, in scenarios where subproblems were not yet solved to optimality within the time budget, decomposition consistently outperformed monolithic solving, demonstrating that the approach better utilizes the same limited computation time. However, decomposition cannot improve beyond this structural optimum imposed by the information asymmetry at stage boundaries. Monolithic solving can eventually find better solutions given extra time. Based on these observations, stage decomposition should primarily be applied to large scenarios and/or stage-aligned supply chains with balanced complexity across stages. Decomposition should be avoided for production-dominated scenarios where discrete variables concentrate in production while procurement and distribution are comparatively simple, as well as scenarios with high resource sharing.

#heading(level: 2, outlined: false)[Solution Quality Assessment]

The evaluation reveals a fundamental problem regarding solution quality assessment. Decomposition provides no global optimality bound because the bounds from individual subproblems do not compose into a valid bound for the original monolithic problem. Therefore, practitioners cannot determine the optimality gap of the decomposed solution.

However, this could also impose an advantage, since a large portion of monolithic solve time typically goes toward proving optimality rather than finding good solutions @bixby2012. Decomposition mitigates this by requiring optimality proofs only for individual subproblems rather than the full problem. Given the non-linear scaling of optimality proofs, proving smaller subproblems optimal requires less time than proving the monolithic problem optimal.

#heading(level: 2, outlined: false)[Limitations]

Several implementation limitations affect the scope and generalizability of this work.

The stage decomposition approach introduces computational overhead for graph analysis, subproblem construction, coordination logic and result assembly. For the evaluation, time used for these computations was not reported. For large scenarios where solve time dominates total runtime, this overhead is negligible. For smaller scenarios that solve quickly monolithically, the overhead can exceed the solve time benefits. The evaluation did not systematically quantify this overhead or identify the problem size threshold where decomposition becomes beneficial, as discussed and justified in @methodology.

Certain constraint types in the SAP IBP optimization model cannot be decomposed within the current framework and scenarios containing those were excluded from the analysis. Late delivery requires global information to determine which link node demands will ultimately be mapped to which customer demands. Initial inventory of materials prevents the application of dead-end detection, lead time aggregation and marginal cost heuristics, because they rely on proportional relations. Minimum aggregated constraints cannot be partitioned because the behavior of other subnetworks cannot be predicted at decomposition time and therefore the constraint cannot be decomposed meaningfully. Because of the listed reasons, scenarios using these features are not supported.

The evaluation focused exclusively on stage decomposition with three fixed stage groups corresponding to procurement, production and distribution. Alternative decomposition strategies exist that partition along different dimensions, for example, product decomposition. Product decomposition creates subproblems per product family. The evaluation does not compare stage decomposition against product decomposition, leaving open whether stage boundaries represent the most effective partition strategy for supply chain optimization. Furthermore, hybrid approaches combining stage decomposition with product decomposition could potentially achieve better results than any single decomposition dimension.

The benchmark scenarios, while drawn from real customer data at SAP, represent a convenience sample rather than a representative sample of all supply chain optimization problems. The scenarios vary in size and structure but share common characteristics imposed by the SAP IBP modeling framework. Decomposition performance on supply chains modeled in different frameworks or with fundamentally different structures may differ from the observed results.